UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 2: Digital basics and data', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Digital systems represent data with bits (0 or 1). Eight bits make one byte — a common small chunk for text characters and tiny files. Bigger files use thousands (KB) or millions (MB) of bytes. Exact sizes vary by format.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same technology/design idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nOrder from smallest to largest typical size: 1 bit, 1 byte, 1 megabyte photo.\n\nFEEDBACK\n1 bit < 1 byte (8 bits) < 1 megabyte photo.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking a bit and a byte are the same size. A byte is a group of bits.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nExplain to a Year 6 student the difference between a bit and a byte in two sentences, with one size comparison to a photo.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation, criterion or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Information and Software Technology","learning_intention":"Digital systems represent data with bits (0 or 1). Eight bits make one byte — a common small chunk for text characters and tiny files. Bigger files use thousands (KB) or millions (MB) of bytes. Exact sizes vary by format.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe classroom tools/materials under teacher supervision","Device only when the lesson requires digital work"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["IST5-DAT-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Explain to a Year 6 student the difference between a bit and a byte in two sentences, with one size comparison to a photo. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using bit on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking a bit and a byte are the same size.. Correction: 1 bit < 1 byte (8 bits) < 1 megabyte photo.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking a bit and a byte are the same size. A byte is a group of bits. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating bit on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with bit, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about bit with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for bit. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"1 bit < 1 byte (8 bits) < 1 megabyte photo is correct because This matches the taught method for bit. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy the model objects and call that independent work","1 bit < 1 byte (8 bits) < 1 megabyte photo","Thinking a bit and a byte are the same size","Ignore the observation or criteria and invent a more exciting result"],"question":"Order from smallest to largest typical size: 1 bit, 1 byte, 1 megabyte photo. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for bit; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of byte with clear evidence","Thinking a bit and a byte are the same size","Digital systems represent data with bits (0 or 1)","1 bit < 1 byte (8 bits) < 1 megabyte photo"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with bit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named byte, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply bit to new material and name the byte used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Thinking a bit and a byte are the same size","Name byte only, without linking it to bit"],"question":"Which response best applies bit with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence appropriately for this lesson on bit. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["1 bit < 1 byte (8 bits) < 1 megabyte photo","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing","Thinking a bit and a byte are the same size"],"question":"Which response best evaluates design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence when analysing bit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Call the first attempt finished without testing","Thinking a bit and a byte are the same size","Name a criterion, safety step or test result and say how it improves bit","Start making with no criteria or safety check"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about bit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Digital systems represent data with bits (0 or 1).) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing","The letter A in simple encoding can be stored in one byte","Thinking a bit and a byte are the same size"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about bit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check criteria and safety, record a test result, and note one honest improvement","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete"],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for bit?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name byte only, with no linked example","Explain to a Year 6 student the difference between a bit and a byte in two sentences, with one size comparison to a","Retype the worked example with no new example","Thinking a bit and a byte are the same size"],"question":"Which independent response best applies bit to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about bit with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating bit on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using bit on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for bit. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with bit, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking a bit and a byte are the same size.. Correction: 1 bit < 1 byte (8 bits) < 1 megabyte photo.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-information-software-technology-y9-w01-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Year 7 IST alignment. Official outcome codes must match the published IST syllabus page; catalogue codes preferred when present. Verification pending. Not a hacking guide. Follow school acceptable-use rules.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise bit.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Information and Software Technology","success_criteria":["I can use bit with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify byte and explain why it matters.","I can practise digital independently on a new prompt.","I can notice represent needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Digital systems represent data with bits (0 or 1).","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Digital systems represent data with bits (0 or 1)"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about bit?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThe letter A in simple encoding can be stored in one byte. A short plain-text note of 200 characters is roughly 200 bytes before formatting — much smaller than a photo of several MB.\n\nTRY FIRST\nOrder from smallest to largest typical size: 1 bit, 1 byte, 1 megabyte photo.\n\nFEEDBACK\n1 bit < 1 byte (8 bits) < 1 megabyte photo.","explain":"Digital systems represent data with bits (0 or 1). Eight bits make one byte — a common small chunk for text characters and tiny files. Bigger files use thousands (KB) or millions (MB) of bytes. Exact sizes vary by format.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking a bit and a byte are the same size. A byte is a group of bits. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"A byte groups eight bits.","caption":"Bits build bytes; files use many bytes.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Bit</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">0/1</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Byte</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">8 bits</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: The letter A in simple encoding can be stored in one byte. A short plain-text note of 200 characters is roughly 200 byte Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: The letter A in simple encoding can be stored in one byte. A short plain-text note of 200 characters is roughly 200 byte Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about bit?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThe letter A in simple encoding can be stored in one byte. A short plain-text note of 200 characters is roughly 200 bytes before formatting — much smaller than a photo of several MB. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show technology thinking. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"Photos usually dwarf plain text in size.","caption":"Sizes are typical, not exact for every file.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Text note</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">small</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Photo</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">MB</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no evidence","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about bit?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking a bit and a byte are the same size. A byte is a group of bits. Here is a more productive model: The letter A in simple encoding can be stored in one byte. A short plain-text note of 200 characters is roughly 200 bytes before formatting — much smaller than a photo of several MB.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\n1 bit < 1 byte (8 bits) < 1 megabyte photo.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Thinking a bit and a byte are the same size. A byte is a group of bits. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about bit?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDigital systems represent data with bits (0 or 1). Eight bits make one byte — a common small chunk for text characters and tiny files. Bigger files use thousands (KB) or millions (MB) of bytes. Exact sizes vary by format. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same technology idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 2: Digital basics and data","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Digital basics and data","week":1,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nThe letter A in simple encoding can be stored in one byte. A short plain-text note of 200 characters is roughly 200 bytes before formatting — much smaller than a photo of several MB.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, criteria, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A byte groups eight bits.","caption":"Bits build bytes; files use many bytes.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Bit</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">0/1</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Byte</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">8 bits</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Photos usually dwarf plain text in size.","caption":"Sizes are typical, not exact for every file.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Text note</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">small</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Photo</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">MB</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-information-software-technology-y9-w01-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
